    "description": "- Utilized Sprint Planning Process, and worked closely with another engineer to tailor the tool's input and output, synchronize with his tool and automate the entire process of setup as well as scheduling tests, and analysis. This process was later deployed in the US and Bangalore for all WHQL testing purposes, due to its enhanced reliability and minimal manual intervention.\n\n- Managed multiple testbeds running at once, each of which could be switched to Windows 10, 8.1, 8, and 7, either 32-bit or 64-bit.  \n\n- Oversaw several WHCK testbed transitions, including Windows XP to Vista, Vista to Windows 7, Windows 7 to Windows 8, Windows 8 to Windows 8.1, Windows 8.1 to Windows 10. Worked with these early versions of Windows and the newest test kits to identify potential issues, and gather logs to determine whether the issue was derived from the driver side or the customer's operating system side.\n\n- Collaborated with developers to monitor and identify new issues, develop debug logs, and test feasible solutions.\n\n- Wrote a program using C# and Microsoft's Object Model to be able to run WHQL using command line commands, rather than the WHCK GUI. Included a help file, and roughly 30 different commands. \n\n- Used C# instead of Powershell, due to expected length of the code and easier debugging when built with a compiler.\n\n- This effort helped increased reliability and eliminated the need for manual intervention.\n",
